    <div class="head">
      <span class="cat-tag">Vacuums</span>
      <h1>Best Robot Vacuums: What $200, $380, and $900 Actually Buy</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated Nov 2026 · Prices checked Nov 27, 2026 · US models only</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lede">
      <p>
        <strong>The Roborock Q7 M5+ is the robot vacuum most people should buy.</strong> It's
        Wirecutter's current top pick, it empties itself into a 2.7-liter bag for about seven weeks,
        and on Nov 27 Roborock's US store listed it at $199.99 against a $429.99 list price — roughly
        what a decent cordless stick vacuum costs. What it can't do is see: there's no camera on the
        front, so it bumps its way around clutter instead of steering around it. If pet hair on
        carpet is the actual problem, RTINGS' head-to-head testing puts the <strong>MOVA P50 Pro
        Ultra</strong> ahead of every flagship it compared for about $380. And if your sofa and bed
        sit low enough that most robots simply can't get under them, the <strong>Roborock Saros
        10R</strong> is 3.14 inches tall with no lidar tower on top — the reason to pay $899.99 while
        it's on sale.
      </p>
    </div>


    <h2 class="block-title">Everything we recommend</h2>
    <div class="picks">
      <a class="pick" href="#q7m5">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best overall</span>
        <span class="pick-role">Wirecutter's top pick, 7-week self-empty dock</span>
        <span class="pick-name">Roborock Q7 M5+</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$199.99 · Roborock (list $429.99)</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#p50">
        <span class="pick-tag alt">Best for pet hair</span>
        <span class="pick-role">RTINGS: beats the flagship on carpet and pet hair</span>
        <span class="pick-name">MOVA P50 Pro Ultra</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$379 · Amazon (list $799)</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#saros">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best under low furniture</span>
