        <span class="pick-price">$899.99 · Roborock (list $1,599.99)</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ 1. Q7 M5+ ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="q7m5">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best overall</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">1. Roborock Q7 M5+</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Suction</span><b>10,000 Pa (Roborock claim, Max+)</b></div>
        <div><span>Navigation</span><b>PreciSense lidar, multi-level maps</b></div>
        <div><span>Obstacle avoidance</span><b>None — bumper and map only</b></div>
        <div><span>Dock</span><b>RockDock Plus · 2.7 L bag · ~7 weeks</b></div>
        <div><span>Runtime</span><b>150 min claimed (3,200 mAh)</b></div>
        <div><span>Mop</span><b>Passive drag pad, no lift</b></div>
        <div><span>Height / weight</span><b>~4 in body · 10.58 lb</b></div>
        <div><span>Warranty</span><b>1 year</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Wirecutter's current top pick, called one of its most thorough cleaners and best navigators</li>
            <li>$199.99 on Roborock's US store on Nov 27 — $230 below its $429.99 list price</li>
            <li>Dual anti-tangle brush and side brush; owners with heavy-shedding dogs report it copes</li>
            <li>Self-empty dock holds a 2.7 L bag, about seven weeks of debris</li>
            <li>Lidar mapping with room splits, per-room suction and multi-floor maps</li>
            <li>10,000 Pa is a lot of suction for the money</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>No camera, so no real obstacle avoidance — RTINGS rated its obstacle handling poor</li>
            <li>The mop pad drags and can't lift, so it wets carpet unless you pull the module off</li>
            <li>Small battery: owners in 1,700-plus-square-foot homes report mid-clean trips back to the dock</li>
            <li>Proprietary bags run $31.99 for six, and the dock needs one, not a tip-out bin</li>
            <li>One-year warranty, and support hold times draw complaints</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          The Q7 M5+ is the value story of the category right now, and almost none of that is about
          suction numbers. Wirecutter's guide names it the top pick for most homes, describing it as
          one of the most thorough cleaners and one of the best navigators in its test group, with an
          app that schedules rooms, blocks off areas and sends the robot back to spots it skipped. The
          dock sucks the bin empty into a sealed bag, which is the feature that actually changes daily
          life: you touch the robot roughly every seven weeks instead of every two days.
        </p>
        <p>
          Independent testing backs the cleaning claims. RTINGS found excellent hard-floor pickup and
          good pet-hair removal from carpet, with the vast majority gone in a single pass. TechRadar
          cleared oats and most of a deliberately awkward tea-leaf test from carpet in three passes,
          and T3 found hair, crumbs and debris gone on both floor types. Every one of those reviewers
          also found the same weakness: clutter. RTINGS rated obstacle handling poor, with cords and
          tasselled rugs tripping it up; TechRadar watched it plough into a cable, socks and a dog
          bowl. It doesn't learn your floor, so the honest workflow is a 60-second pickup before each
          run.
        </p>
        <p>
          Owner reports split along floor area, not quality. People in 1,100 to 1,400 square feet
          describe a mapped home cleaned in a couple of hours; people in 1,700 square feet or more
          describe two or three charging trips, and one owner returned a unit bought for an
          1,100-square-foot condo. The battery is 3,200 mAh against a 150-minute claim, so treat this
          as a one-level, sub-1,500-square-foot robot. Two other things worth knowing: the mop is a damp
          pad on a detachable module, fine for paw prints and useless on dried stains, and the app draws
          recurring complaints about map editing and Wi-Fi reconnection — both home testers hit glitches.
          One retailer's buyer summary also claims this model has no cliff detection, which contradicts
          Roborock's stated spec sheet; that claim is unresolved, so if you have a stairwell, verify it
          before buying or put up a physical barrier.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the cheapest robot here that most people will actually be happy with — provided your floors are one level and you'll pick up the socks.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 2. MOVA P50 Pro Ultra ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="p50">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best for pet hair and carpet</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">2. MOVA P50 Pro Ultra</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Suction</span><b>19,000 Pa (MOVA claim)</b></div>
        <div><span>Navigation</span><b>Lidar + RGB camera, 3D maps, dirt detect</b></div>
        <div><span>Mop</span><b>Dual rotating pads, 10 mm lift, auto-detach</b></div>
        <div><span>Dock</span><b>75°C pad wash, warm-air dry, 3 L bag · 75 days</b></div>
        <div><span>Threshold climb</span><b>0.7 in (RTINGS measurement)</b></div>
        <div><span>Runtime</span><b>5,200 mAh; 210–260 min claimed</b></div>
        <div><span>Weight</span><b>~30.3 lb with dock</b></div>
        <div><span>Warranty</span><b>3 years</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>RTINGS calls it the best mid-range robot vacuum it has tested</li>
            <li>RTINGS' direct comparison: it "significantly outperforms" the Saros 10R on pet hair and carpet</li>
            <li>Vacuum Wars measured 0% hair wrap on the brush versus a 38% category average</li>
            <li>75°C hot-water pad wash plus warm-air drying — no damp mop smell</li>
            <li>75-day dock cycle and a 3-year warranty, the longest of the three</li>
            <li>$379 on Amazon on Nov 27 against a $799 list price</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>RTINGS found it unreliable at spotting pet waste on carpet — run it while you're home</li>
            <li>Only a 0.7-inch threshold climb per RTINGS; thick rugs and door bars can stop it</li>
            <li>MOVA is the newest brand in the US here, with a thinner parts and app-support record</li>
            <li>Front-wheel durability questions carry over from the P10 Pro, per owner reports</li>
            <li>Several Amazon listings at different prices and bundles — check the seller and what's in the box</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          RTINGS is blunt about this one: it's the best robot vacuum it has tested at a mid-range
          price, and in a direct comparison it says the P50 "significantly outperforms" Roborock's
          flagship Saros 10R on pet hair pickup and carpet cleaning. That's the single most useful
          sentence in this whole category, because it inverts the usual assumption that the $1,600
          machine wins everywhere. If you have a shedder and rugs, the cheaper robot is the better
          robot.
        </p>
        <p>
          The mechanism is mechanical rather than clever software. A fixed comb sits above the main
          roller and shears hair loose before it can wind on, which is why Vacuum Wars recorded 0%
          wrap against a 38% category average in its tangle test. The dock is the other half of the
          pitch: it washes the pads at 75°C, dries them warm, auto-empties into a 3-liter bag rated for
          about 75 days, refills the clean tank and doses detergent. The pads lift 10 mm and detach
          automatically when the robot detects carpet, so you're not dragging a wet pad across a rug.
        </p>
        <p>
          The caveats are about maturity, not performance. The threshold climb is the weak point —
          RTINGS measured 0.7 inches, against 1.5 inches for the Saros 10R — so a deep-pile rug edge or
          a raised door bar can strand it. MOVA is also the newest name in the US market of the three
          brands here, a Dreame sub-brand with a shorter track record on parts availability and app
          support, and owner threads on the sibling P10 Pro raise front-wheel durability questions that
          this model inherits by design. Amazon's rating sits around 4.0, which is respectable rather
          than glowing. Buy from a retailer with a generous return window.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> if pet hair on carpet is the reason you're shopping, this is the one — and it costs a quarter of the flagship.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 3. Saros 10R ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="saros">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best under low furniture</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">3. Roborock Saros 10R</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Height</span><b>3.14 in (79.8 mm)</b></div>
        <div><span>Navigation</span><b>StarSight 2.0 — 3D ToF + RGB + VertiBeam</b></div>
        <div><span>Suction</span><b>22,000 Pa (Roborock claim)</b></div>
        <div><span>Mop</span><b>Dual rotating pads, auto-detach on carpet</b></div>
        <div><span>Dock 4.0</span><b>80°C pad wash, hot-air dry, 2.5 L bag</b></div>
        <div><span>Threshold climb</span><b>1.5 in (RTINGS measurement)</b></div>
        <div><span>Battery</span><b>6,400 mAh · 180 min · ~2.5 h charge</b></div>
        <div><span>Wireless</span><b>2.4 GHz Wi-Fi only</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>RTINGS' best robot vacuum tested overall in its 2026 guide</li>
            <li>3.14 inches tall with no lidar turret — it fits under sofas, platform beds and toe kicks</li>
            <li>Highest threshold climb here at 1.5 inches</li>
            <li>Dock washes pads at 80°C, dries them, empties, refills and doses detergent</li>
            <li>108 object types recognised, plus a side sensor aimed along walls and furniture edges</li>
            <li>180-minute runtime and a 2.5-hour charge</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>$1,599.99 list; the $899.99 price is a sale that has moved between roughly $899 and $1,100 this fall</li>
            <li>Carpet results conflict badly between testers — see below</li>
            <li>Time-of-flight navigation can be confused by very dark rugs and black furniture legs</li>
            <li>2.4 GHz Wi-Fi only, no 5 GHz</li>
            <li>The dock is large and needs floor space plus clearance above it</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          The Saros 10R's most important specification is a dimension. At 3.14 inches it clears the
          gap under a platform bed, a low media console and a kitchen toe kick — the places where a
          robot with a lidar puck on top simply stops. Roborock gets there by removing the turret
          entirely and navigating with StarSight 2.0: a 3D time-of-flight sensor, an RGB camera and a
          side-facing VertiBeam sensor that watches walls and furniture edges. RTINGS names it the best
          robot vacuum it has tested overall in its 2026 guide, and RTINGS measured a 1.5-inch
          threshold climb, the best of the three here.
        </p>
        <p>
          The dock is genuinely hands-off: 80°C pad washing, hot-air drying, auto-empty into a 2.5-liter
          bag, auto-refill, detergent dosing and a self-cleaning base. Pads spin at up to 200 rpm with
          30 water levels in the app and detach automatically for carpet runs. Realistically, that's the
          difference between a chore and an appliance — but you pay for it in footprint, and this dock
          needs a wall.
        </p>
        <p>
          Carpet is where the sources stop agreeing, and it's worth being explicit. Tom's Guide
          reported 98% pickup on carpet and 100% on hardwood; TechGearLab reported only 56% carpet
          pickup for the same model; RTINGS' own comparison says the MOVA P50 Pro Ultra beats it on
          pet hair and carpet. Those numbers can't all describe the same conditions, and the
          discrepancy is unresolved. What is consistent across sources is that time-of-flight sensing
          struggles with very dark rugs and low-contrast obstacles, and that the robot is cautious
          rather than fast in a cluttered room. If carpet is your main floor, this is not the pick —
          the MOVA is, and it's cheaper.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> buy it for the height and the dock, not for the badge — and only while it's discounted.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Also worth knowing</h2>
      <p>
        Three names that come up constantly and just missed the cut. The <strong>Tapo RV30 Max
        Plus</strong> ($290 at TP-Link) is Wirecutter's runner-up: it navigates smoothly, cleans open
        space well and self-empties, but it has less suction, a less flexible app and isn't as thorough
        in corners, all on a one-year warranty. The <strong>eufy Auto-Empty C10</strong> is RTINGS'
        cheap pick — a 2.85-inch body, a 3-liter bag and a 4,000 Pa rating, with no mopping system at
        all, which for some buyers is a feature. And the <strong>Narwal Freo X10 Pro</strong> ($549.99
        list) is Wirecutter's top vacuum-and-mop combo, praised for wet-mess tests; its mop-lift spec
        is listed as both 10 mm and 12 mm depending on the source, and its obstacle avoidance is
        mid-tier. Treat all three as leads rather than picks — none has been cross-checked here against
        primary lab data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">How we picked</h2>
      <p>
        The shortlist comes from Wirecutter's current robot vacuum guide and RTINGS' 2026 rankings and
        head-to-head comparisons, cross-checked against Tom's Guide, TechRadar and T3 hands-on testing
        and Vacuum Wars' tangle measurements. Specs and prices were verified on Roborock's and MOVA's
        US pages and on Amazon on <strong>Nov 27, 2026</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Before you buy: two things almost nobody tells you</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>"Self-emptying" is not "hands-free."</strong> The dock empties the bin; you still buy
        bags, pull hair off the roller, refill the clean tank, dump the dirty one and rinse the
        washboard. Budget for consumables — Roborock's six-pack of bags is $31.99, and at a
        seven-week cycle that's most of a year — and budget for floor space: a dock needs a wall, a
        power outlet and clearance above it, and it will not fit under a kitchen cabinet overhang.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Measure the gap, not the robot.</strong> A 3.14-inch body still needs about 3.3 inches
        of actual clearance to pass a sofa leg or a platform-bed frame, and you have to measure the
        lowest obstruction along the path, not the tallest. The same goes for thresholds: a 0.7-inch
        climb rating means a deep-pile rug edge or a raised door bar can stop a robot that looks
        perfectly capable on the spec sheet. Buy somewhere with a real return window — every model here
        performs differently in a 900-square-foot apartment than in a two-storey house, and returns are
        the only test that counts for your floor plan.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

